# Everything below is GENERATED by running this project. None of it belongs in
# git — it is either large, machine-specific, or a snapshot of a site that will
# have changed by the time anyone reads it. A single 3-page run drops several
# MB of HTML snapshots alone.

# --- scrape output ---------------------------------------------------------
*.json
*.csv
!package.json
# Committed on purpose: a real excerpt of a real run (--mode category),
# so a reader can see the exact output shape without installing anything.
!sample_output.json
# The sample's run-metadata sidecar travels with it. `.github/ci_checks.py
# --run-check` reads it, so a fresh checkout has to carry it or `--all`
# fails on a file the repo deliberately withheld.
!sample_output.meta.json
!sample_output.csv
bershka_products.*
bershka_products_*.*
# The canary workflow's own run, and anyone reproducing it locally.
canary_run.*

# --- debug artifacts and raw dumps ------------------------------------------
# Matched by KIND rather than by one remembered name: a raw capture reaches
# `git add -A` through whatever directory someone happened to call it, so
# every usual name for a scratch or output directory is covered, plus any
# bare HTML file at the repo root and every per-page dump shape
# (`x.page3`, `x.json.page3`). Large, stale within hours, and a capture
# carries whatever per-visitor state the page held.
*_debug.html
*_debug.png
*.html.page*
*.page[0-9]*
/*.html
live/
live_results/
out/
output/
run/
runs/
result/
results/
tmp/
scratch/
dump/
dumps/

# --- a downloaded 2captcha proxy list is a credential file ------------------
# Every line of one carries a login and a password. The glob covers the
# variants people actually make (proxylist-us.txt, proxylist.2.txt) and
# stops there: a broader shape such as *proxy*.txt would also match a file
# someone meant to commit, and .gitignore has no undo. Every proxy filename
# this project's own documentation shows must match this -- checked in the
# suite with `git check-ignore`, not by comparing strings, because
# .gitignore has patterns, negations and directory scoping and a substring
# match proves nothing.
proxylist*.txt

# --- caches ------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
# fingerprint_client.py's own on-disk cache defaults to
# ~/.cache/2captcha-fingerprints (outside the repo) -- this only matters if
# --cache-dir ever points somewhere inside it.
.fingerprint_cache/

# --- packaging build artifacts ----------------------------------------------
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# --- environments ------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/

# --- editors / OS --------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- never, under any circumstances --------------------------------------
# The scrapers mask user:pass in their own logs, but raw HTML dumps are NOT
# masked, and a key pasted into a shell history file is not masked either.
*.log
*.key
secrets*
# Every copy of the credentials file -- `.env.bak`, `.env.local`,
# `.env.save` -- not only `.env` itself: a renamed working copy is exactly
# the one nobody remembers is there. `.env.example` is documentation and is
# committed.
.env
.env*
!.env.example

# --- local research material ------------------------------------------------
# `captures/` holds the raw responses this repo was built against (storefront
# HTML, menu, grid and productsArray JSON from www.bershka.com). It is
# EVIDENCE, kept on disk so a claim in the README can be re-checked, but it
# is not repo content -- the fixtures the test suite actually runs against
# are trimmed, scrubbed excerpts embedded in smoke_test.py. A committed
# capture would also carry whatever per-visitor state the page happened to
# hold.
captures/
